@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' purpose: how codex is steered and gated inside the container — the instruction tiers, the read-only review split, and why its own sandbox is off
' covers: defs/codex/entrypoint.sh
' covers: packages/lib/entrypoint-lib.sh
' covers: defs/codex/defaults/config.toml
' covers: defs/codex/Dockerfile
' covers: internal/imagebuild/targets.go
' covers: internal/contract/agents_shared_test.go
' covers: internal/imagetest/codex_test.go
'   the def's resolved topology ... _spec/defs/codex/codex-topology.puml
'
' ── WHAT TestImageCodex HOLDS THE ENTRYPOINT TO (internal/imagetest/codex_test.go)
' Each rule below is one or more checks there; the launch rules run through a
' fake `codex` on PATH that prints the argv the entrypoint built.
'   * The preamble names the paradigm, so a transcript says which loop ran.
'   * No credential of either kind (no login, no OPENAI_API_KEY): the
'     entrypoint says so rather than launching into a login prompt nobody is
'     there to answer.
'   * Utility subcommands (login, logout, …) do agent-free work, so they get no
'     sandbox posture.
'   * --json is an `exec` flag, not a global one. It must follow the subcommand,
'     or clap rejects it and the run never starts. A caller's own --json is a
'     parse contract and is not doubled.
'   * PROVEO_AGENT_EVIDENCE=default buys no extra narration.
'   * The WORKSPACE is never written. codex reads AGENTS.md natively, so unlike
'     claudecode there is no bridge file to seed into the operator's checkout,
'     and nothing else may appear there either. A repo steered through
'     CLAUDE.md is still read (the fallback in codex-topology.puml).
'   * The baked defaults are the harness's, not the agent's. The agent may seed
'     FROM them and edit its own $CODEX_HOME copy, but it must not rewrite the
'     source under /opt.
'
' ── IMAGE FACTS defs/codex/Dockerfile STATES WITHOUT ARGUING (N14) ──────────
' /home/agent is the REAL home and /home/codex the alias, for the reason
' _spec/_paradigms/runtime-user-boundary.puml gives: sbx's kits chown
' agent:agent and mount block volumes UNDER /home/agent, and a mount target
' cannot resolve through a symlink. With a symlinked home the volumes land
' where $HOME never points — including the session state `codex resume` reads.
' The alias stays because healthchecks and def tests read paths through it,
' and nothing ever mounts there.
' NODE_ENV is NOT baked, for the reason harness-image-layers.puml gives for
' claudecode: it would make every workspace `npm`/`pnpm install` skip the
' devDependencies (tsc, eslint, test runners) the agent needs. CODEX_HOME is
' not baked either — codex-topology.puml, the note on $CODEX_HOME.
' The daemon is the sbx template's, inherited through proveo/base: the image
' ships no docker binaries of its own since N14 deleted its static-tarball
' layer (2026-10-17; codex-topology.puml, image-size-reduction.puml Cut 4).
title codex paradigm — ML blackbox algorithm (spec → plan → verify loop)

actor "Developer" as DEV

node "codex container" as CTR <<host>> {
  component "defs/codex/entrypoint.sh" as EP <<app>>
  component "codex --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox" as CLI <<app>>

  frame "Instruction tiers (lowest first)" as TIERS <<host>> {
    component "$CODEX_HOME/AGENTS.md\nproveo house rules" as HOUSE <<app>>
    component "AGENTS.md in the repo\nread natively" as PROJ <<app>>
  }

  frame "In-loop review gates" as GATES <<host>> {
    component "adversarial-reviewer\nsecurity-reviewer\n(sandbox_mode = read-only)" as RO <<app>>
    component "spec-keeper\n(workspace-write — the single writer)" as SK <<app>>
  }

  database "/app — repo" as WS <<db>>
}

node "Layer 2 — sbx sandbox + its network policy" as L2 <<host>> {
  component "sbx microVM\n(the hardened docker container until 2026-10-16)" as SBX <<app>>
  component "sbx network policy + credential proxy\n(proveo's open · allowlist · review tiers retired)" as EG <<app>>
}

cloud "api.openai.com" as VENDOR <<cloud>>

DEV ARROW_MAIN CLI
CLI ARROW_MAIN HOUSE
HOUSE ARROW_MAIN PROJ : errorLabel(project outranks house)
CLI ARROW_MAIN RO : final diff, before "done"
RO ARROW_ERROR CLI : errorLabel([BLOCKER]/[HIGH] block completion)
SK ARROW_MAIN WS : _spec/ · PLAN.md · AGENTS.md only
CLI ARROW_MAIN WS
CTR ARROW_MAIN SBX
SBX ARROW_CLOUD VENDOR : via EG

note right of TIERS
  **codex has no managed-policy tier, so the house rules sit at the
  BOTTOM — and that is the honest place for them.**

  claudecode bakes proveo's conventions to
  ""/etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md"", a tier that loads before everything
  else and cannot be excluded at any settings layer. codex has no
  equivalent path. What it has instead is native ""AGENTS.md"": the
  repo's own file is read with no bridge written into the operator's
  checkout, and it ranks ABOVE the global one.

  So the house rules ADD to a repository's rules rather than
  competing with them, and a project can overrule the house. Writing
  them into the workspace to win the ranking was rejected: it mutates
  the operator's tree, and cannot apply where an ""AGENTS.md""
  already exists.
end note

note bottom of GATES
  **The read-only split is what makes a review gate a gate.**

  This harness launches with approvals off, so an advisor that could
  edit would be able to fix its own findings and mark itself green.

  codex exposes no per-tool allowlist — there is no ""tools:"" line to
  write — so the control is each agent's ""sandbox_mode"", set to
  ""read-only"". It is the same guarantee claudecode gets from its
  frontmatter, expressed in the only vocabulary codex enforces.
  ""spec-keeper"" is the single writer, scoped by its prompt to
  ""_spec/"", ""PLAN.md"" and ""AGENTS.md"".
end note

note bottom of CLI
  **The CLI is installed system-wide from npm, and pinnable.**
  ""@openai/codex"" ships the same native binary as the install script,
  but a bare ""curl | sh"" resolves whatever release is current, which
  is not a thing an image can reproduce. ""--codex-version"" pins it.

  **Evidence is limited by the CLI, not by policy.** The TUI has no
  verbosity switch; the only dial is ""codex exec --json"", which emits
  each event as JSONL. A caller's own ""--json"" is a parse contract and
  wins. So verbose here buys structured events in headless runs and
  nothing interactively — which the preamble says out loud
  rather than implying it bought something.
end note

note bottom of L2
  **The sbx sandbox is the boundary, and sbx's network policy decides
  egress** (_spec/internal/sbx/policy-baseline\.puml; proveo's egress tiers
  were retired 2026-10-16). ""api.openai.com"" is a service sbx's proxy
  brokers, and the manifest constrains only the provider
  (""providers: [openai]"").

  Unlike cursor's Landlock, codex's own sandbox COULD run here. It is
  off because it confines the agent to part of a tree that is already
  a disposable copy while its prompts stall an unattended loop —
  a cost with no matching benefit, not an impossibility.
  ""PROVEO_CODEX_SANDBOX"" turns it back on per run.

  **The caller's own posture wins, and it has to: passing both is
  fatal, not redundant.** proveo appends its flag only when the argv
  carries none of codex's own approval or sandbox flags, because clap
  reads the pair as a CONFLICT and fails the run outright rather than
  degrading to one of the two. A posture proveo cannot supply is
  therefore always reachable by supplying it directly.
end note

@enduml
